    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 28 January 1930 the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-primo-de-rivera">Miguel Primo de Rivera</a>, in power since September 1923, was overthrown. Republicans of many backgrounds, conservatives, socialists and Catalan nationalists among them, joined forces in the Pact of San Sebastián, which committed them to overthrowing the monarchy and allowed a joint campaign against it. The municipal elections of 12 April 1931 gave the republicans a landslide victory. Two days later the Second Republic was proclaimed, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> went into exile, and a provisional government was formed under Niceto Alcalá-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>. On 11 May Catholic churches and buildings in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and Sevilla were set on fire.</p><p>In June 1931 a Constituent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> was elected to draft a constitution, which came into force in December. The Radical and Socialist majority then postponed regular elections for two more years, and the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> began numerous reforms in what it saw as a modernisation of the country. These two years are known as the Reformist Biennium. The Republic adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a> as its national anthem and a tricolour of red, yellow and purple as the flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Second Spanish Republic&#8221; cites where it covers Assassinations of political leaders and beginning of the war:</p><ul><li>Stanley G. Payne, The Spanish Civil War (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li><li>Stanley G. Payne, Franco: A Personal and Political Biography (University of Wisconsin Press, 2018)</li><li>Stanley G. Payne, The Franco regime, 1936–1975 (University of Wisconsin Press, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic's economy was mostly agrarian, and many historians call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in these years a "backward nation". Its major industries lay in the Basque region, which had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s best high-grade non-phosphoric ore, and in Catalonia, on the opposite side of the country from its resources, so that the mountainous terrain made transport very costly. Exports were low and manufacturing worked mainly for the home market. High levels of poverty left many Spaniards open to extremist parties.</p><p>The first governments carried out social reforms: a legal right to seven days of paid annual leave, the freedom to strike without fear of dismissal, new social security benefits and an eight-hour working day. A moderate agrarian reform expropriated landowners, and after the failed coup of the Sanjurjada it was made more radical against the aristocracy. In 1932 religious orders were forbidden to run schools while the government began a large school-building project. The Constitution allowed divorce, stripped the nobility of any special legal status and laid down procedures for nationalising public services, land, banks and railways. The land reforms were suspended in the "black biennium" that followed the elections of 1933.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic faced armed risings from both sides. Monarchist generals planned the first coup against it, the Sanjurjada, which failed; several hundred generals were dismissed. An anarchist insurrection ended in the Casas Viejas massacre. In October 1934 the miners of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, killing officials and clergymen, and mobilised thirty thousand workers within ten days. The rising was crushed after two weeks by the army under General Eduardo López Ochoa, known afterwards as the "Butcher of Asturias", which used mainly Moorish colonial troops from Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><p>On 17 July 1936 General Franco led the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armee-d-afrique">Army of Africa</a> from Morocco against the mainland while General Emilio Mola moved south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Most of the main cities stayed loyal, and the rebels began a war of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Portugal gave the rebels much greater and more effective help than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the International <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigades</a> gave the Republic, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept to non-intervention. The Republicans held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> in November 1936 and at Jarama and Guadalajara in 1937, but the north fell in late 1937. The bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> served as a testing ground for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/condor-legion">Condor Legion</a>, and the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> in 1938 was the Republic's last attempt to turn the tide.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Second Spanish Republic&#8221; cites where it covers Assassinations of political leaders and beginning of the war:</p><ul><li>Stanley G. Payne, The Spanish Civil War (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li><li>Stanley G. Payne, Franco: A Personal and Political Biography (University of Wisconsin Press, 2018)</li><li>Stanley G. Payne, The Franco regime, 1936–1975 (University of Wisconsin Press, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Constitution of 1931 created a single chamber, the Congress of Deputies, and guaranteed freedom of speech and association. It ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>'s status as the state religion, and its articles 26 and 27 put strict controls on Church property and barred religious orders from teaching; scholars have called it hostile to religion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> condemned the deprivation of Catholics' civil liberties in the encyclical Dilectissima <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobi">Nobis</a>. Every region had the right to autonomy: Catalonia used it in 1932 and the Basque Country in 1936, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>'s statute never came into effect because of the war.</p><p>Politics was deeply divided. When the CEDA won the elections of 1933, President Alcalá-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> would not let its leader, Gil Robles, form a government and invited Alejandro Lerroux of the Radical Republican Party instead; the CEDA governed indirectly until it won three ministries in October 1934. The left regarded the CEDA as fascist, and Gil Robles praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>, Hitler and Dollfuss. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a>'s victory in February 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-azana">Manuel Azaña</a> formed a government and soon replaced Alcalá-Zamora as president. During the war the Republic had three governments, led by José Giral, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-largo-caballero">Francisco Largo Caballero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-negrin">Juan Negrín</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Second Spanish Republic&#8221; cites where it covers Assassinations of political leaders and beginning of the war:</p><ul><li>Stanley G. Payne, The Spanish Civil War (Cambridge University Press, 2012)</li><li>Stanley G. Payne, Franco: A Personal and Political Biography (University of Wisconsin Press, 2018)</li><li>Stanley G. Payne, The Franco regime, 1936–1975 (University of Wisconsin Press, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Republic's reforms touched the Church, the land, the regions and women's place in politics, and they divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sharply. The Constitution opposed the key beliefs of a right wing rooted in rural areas and stripped the Church of its schools and public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, while churches were burnt in 1931, 1932 and 1933. On the extreme right the Spanish Renovation, the small Falange Española and the Carlist militias grew; the historian Julián Casanova describes the anti-Republican right's culture as proto-fascist. Historians still disagree about the elections of February 1936: Stanley G. Payne and the scholars Manuel Álvarez Tardío and Roberto Villa García argue that there was major fraud, while Eduardo Calleja and Francisco Pérez reply that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> would have won a narrow majority even so.</p><p>The war that ended the Republic became an international event. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> intervened for the rebels, the Non-Intervention Committee isolated the Republic, and the bombing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a>'s painting. In the Republic's territory a social revolution broke out after July 1936, inspired mostly by libertarian socialist, anarchist and communist ideas, with the trade unions of the UGT and the CNT as its main forces.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real<span>1761</span></a><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego<span>1820</span></a><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol<span>1935</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The killing of the anti-fascist Lieutenant José Castillo by Falangist gunmen on 12 July 1936 was followed early on 13 July by the murder of the monarchist leader José Calvo Sotelo by Assault Guards and leftist militiamen; later investigations named the socialist gunman Luis Cuenca. Stanley Payne holds that the killing turned a faltering conspiracy into a revolt, and within hours Franco, who had stayed out of the plotting, committed himself to it. The coup began on 17 July. Bloody purges followed in every area the rebels captured. An estimated half a million people died in the war; some have put the figure as high as a million, but modern research gives 500,000.</p><p>After the north and the Aragon front fell and the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> failed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> fell to the rebels in early 1939. The military coup of Segismundo Casado against Negrín ended Republican resistance, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> fell in March, and the Republic was dissolved on 1 April 1939 after surrendering to the Nationalists. Its government survived in exile, keeping an embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> until 1976, and formally dissolved itself after the restoration of democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1931 to 1939. Its Constitution separated Church and state, gave women the vote and granted regional autonomy, but the reforms and the clashes between left and right split the country, and the military uprising of July 1936 began the civil war that ended with the Republic's surrender to Franco's Nationalists.</em></p>

